Methods

Overview of SegTrace
SegTrace detects similar genomic segments without sequence alignment. It accepts one or more FASTA files, where each file represents one genome assembly. The procedure has six stages: window construction, k-mer sketching, hash-indexed candidate generation, shared-hash and collinearity tests, locus clustering, and per-genome copy-number filtering. Candidate generation is bounded within each shared-hash group and does not enumerate every pair of windows.

Inputs and parameters
SegTrace reads uncompressed or gzip-compressed FASTA files through zlib and kseq. Files receive integer identifiers in command-line order. A sequence receives an integer identifier when its length is at least the window size. Sequence identifiers follow file and read order. The genome label used in the output is the input basename after removal of a compression suffix (.gz or .bgz) and a sequence-file suffix (.fa, .fna, .fasta, .fastq, or .fq).

The default k-mer size k is 17, the scale factor s is 16, and the window size W is 1,024 bp. A step size of zero is replaced with T = floor(W/3), and a minimum valid-base count of zero is replaced with B = floor(W/4). The default minimum copy count c is 2 per input file, and values below 1 are set to 1. The default thread count P is 8. The shared-hash test uses the compile-time identity parameter I_min = 0.8.

Window construction and base filtering
A sequence of length L is skipped when L < W. Otherwise, SegTrace creates a window with index j and interval [jT, jT + W) for each nonnegative integer j that satisfies jT + W <= L. A suffix shorter than W is not represented by a window.

By default, A, C, G, and T in either case are valid bases. The -m option makes lowercase a, c, g, and t invalid. Other symbols, including ambiguous nucleotide codes, are invalid in both modes. SegTrace counts valid bases in each window. A window with fewer than B valid bases retains its coordinate record but receives an empty sketch. It cannot seed a candidate pair.

Canonical k-mer hashing
For each window that passes the valid-base test, SegTrace scans each run of valid bases with ntHash. An invalid base resets the rolling state, so no k-mer spans an invalid base. For each k-mer, ntHash updates a 64-bit forward-strand hash and a 64-bit reverse-complement hash. The smaller value is the canonical hash, which makes the result independent of the represented strand.

The canonical hash is mixed with the seed 42. For a canonical hash x, the implementation applies XOR with the seed, three right-shift XOR operations by 33 bits, and multiplication by 0xa0761d6478bd642f and 0xe7037ed1a0b428db between the shift operations. Arithmetic is performed with 64-bit unsigned integers. The low 32 bits form the sketch hash h.

Scaled hash sampling and sketch storage
SegTrace applies scaled threshold sampling (FracMinHash). It defines the threshold

theta = floor((2^32 - 1)/s)

and retains h when h < theta. If the mixed hashes are uniform, the retained fraction is about 1/s. The implementation stores at most the first 2,048 retained hash occurrences from a window. It sorts these values and removes duplicates. The resulting array is the window sketch.

All sketches are concatenated in one hash array. Each window record stores the offset and number of hashes for its sketch, the sequence identifier, and the window index. Sorting each sketch permits intersection counting by a two-pointer scan.

Hash-indexed candidate generation
SegTrace divides the 32-bit hash range into 1,024 partitions and processes 256 partitions per batch. For each sampled hash, it creates an entry containing the hash and its window identifier. Entries in a partition are sorted by hash and then by window identifier. Entries with the same hash form one run.

A run is processed only when it contains between 2 and 16 windows, inclusive. Runs with more than 16 windows are discarded. Within a retained run, each window is compared with at most the next two windows in window-identifier order. These limits bound work caused by frequent hashes. They also mean that sharing a sampled hash does not guarantee that every possible pair in the run is examined.

Overlap exclusion and Bloom filtering
Before sketch comparison, SegTrace excludes a pair when both windows belong to the same sequence and overlap. For windows a and b with indices j_a and j_b, overlap is defined by

|j_a - j_b|T < W.

This test permits comparisons between nonoverlapping loci on the same sequence and comparisons between different sequences or files.

Each worker thread maintains a Bloom filter with 2^26 bits. The unordered pair of 32-bit window identifiers is encoded as a 64-bit key. A splitmix64 operation supplies two bit positions. If both bits are set, the worker skips the pair. Otherwise, it sets both bits and evaluates the pair. The Bloom filters persist across all partition batches in one run. Because the filters are thread-local, they suppress repeats observed by the same worker but do not provide global deduplication across workers. A Bloom-filter false positive can suppress a pair comparison.

Shared-hash criterion
Let S_a and S_b denote the sorted sketches of two windows, let n_a and n_b denote their sizes, and let H = |S_a intersect S_b|. SegTrace computes H by advancing two pointers through the sorted arrays. Equal values increment H and advance both pointers. Otherwise, the pointer at the smaller value advances.

Under a model in which base matches occur independently with probability I_min, the probability that all k positions of a k-mer match is

p_k = I_min^k.

The implementation uses this value to define the required shared-hash count

Q(a,b) = max(3, ceil(max(n_a, n_b) p_k)).

A window pair passes when H >= Q(a,b). The criterion uses the larger sketch size, not the smaller sketch size. With the default settings, I_min = 0.8 and k = 17. This test is an operational candidate criterion; it is not an alignment-based estimate of sequence identity.

Collinear verification
A pair that passes the shared-hash criterion must have at least one neighboring pair that also passes it. SegTrace tests four direction combinations for the two window arrays: (+,+), (-,-), (+,-), and (-,+). These combinations cover forward and reverse relative order.

The first pass applies equal offsets d to both windows, where 1 <= d <= 8. The second pass applies offsets d_a and d_b, where 1 <= d_a <= 8, 1 <= d_b <= 8, and d_a != d_b. It therefore admits neighboring pairs whose two index offsets differ. A tested neighbor must remain on the same sequence as its corresponding original window, remain within the window array, satisfy the overlap exclusion, and satisfy its own shared-hash threshold. The neighbor is evaluated from its stored sketches and does not need to have been generated by the inverted index. The original pair is retained when any tested neighbor passes.

Candidate score and storage
For a retained pair, SegTrace assigns the score

score(a,b) = floor((255H + floor(max(n_a,n_b)/2))/max(n_a,n_b)).

This is integer rounding of 255H/max(n_a,n_b) to the range 0 through 255. The score is used for partner selection during clustering. It is not written to the output. The implementation packs the score with two 28-bit window identifiers, so one execution can contain at most 2^28 window records. Retained pairs are stored in arrays owned by worker threads.

Duplicate-locus construction
After candidate discovery, SegTrace marks every window that occurs in a retained pair. It then scans marked windows in sequence and coordinate order. For consecutive marked windows on one sequence, the current window joins the preceding locus when j - j_previous <= 8 and starts a new locus otherwise. A marked window on a new sequence starts a new locus. This rule does not require the two window intervals to overlap or to share a candidate edge.

The start of a locus is the start of its first marked window. Its end is the end of its last marked window. The locus therefore includes any unmarked interval between merged windows. Each marked window is mapped to its locus for the clustering step.

Best-partner clustering
SegTrace maps each retained window pair to its two loci. A pair whose endpoints map to the same locus is discarded at this stage. For each locus, SegTrace selects the partner locus from the pair with the highest score. A tie is resolved in favor of the smaller locus index. Partner selection is performed for each endpoint, so two loci do not need to select each other.

SegTrace initializes a Union-Find structure over loci and unions each locus with its selected partner. Union-Find uses union by rank and iterative path splitting. Each connected component of the resulting undirected partner graph receives a cluster identifier. Membership can therefore result from a chain of selected partner edges; it does not imply that every pair of loci in a cluster was compared or passed the shared-hash test. Loci are then sorted by cluster identifier, file identifier, sequence identifier, start, and end.

Per-genome copy-number filtering
Copy number is counted after window-to-locus merging. For each cluster, SegTrace groups loci by input file and retains a file group when it contains at least c loci. A cluster is retained when at least one file group meets this condition. There is no requirement that a cluster occur in two input files. File groups below c are removed even when another file group in the same cluster passes. The retained clusters receive consecutive identifiers in their sorted order.

At this stage, c = 1 retains every cluster and every represented file group. The default c = 2 retains a cluster only if at least one genome contributes two or more loci, and it removes each file group that contributes fewer than two loci. A cluster confined to one input genome passes this filter if and only if that genome contributes at least c loci. The copy count is evaluated before the output length filter.

Parallelization and memory control
Window sketching is parallelized within each sequence. For a sequence of length L, SegTrace starts with a chunk size of max(floor(L/(4P)), 100,000) bases and rounds it up to a multiple of T. Each chunk is extended by W - T bases at its right boundary. This extension permits windows at the boundary to be assigned without omitting a start position. Each job writes hashes and coordinates to its own arrays. The main thread concatenates these arrays in chunk order after all jobs for the sequence finish.

Candidate discovery is parallelized over hash partitions within each batch. Threads own their candidate arrays and Bloom filters, so candidate insertion does not require a shared lock. Each Bloom filter occupies 8 MiB. Partition buckets are released after their batch is processed. Locus construction, partner selection, Union-Find clustering, copy-number filtering, and output are performed serially.

Output
If at least one locus survives copy-number filtering, SegTrace creates <prefix>.seg.bed. If no locus survives, it does not create an output file. The file begins with the header

#chrom\tstart\tend\tcluster_id

and contains four columns. The chrom field is <genome basename>-<FASTA sequence name>. Start and end are zero-based, half-open coordinates. The fourth column is the cluster identifier. The output contains no subcluster identifier, strand, similarity score, or alignment.

Read mode (-r)

Read mode uses exactly the same windowing, sketch comparison, overlap exclusion, collinear verification, locus merging, and Union-Find clustering as genome mode. FASTA/FASTQ records are treated as long-read sequences, so a record can contribute multiple loci.

Pipeline

1. Every eligible window receives the same canonical ntHash sketch used in genome mode.
2. Shared sketch hashes discover candidate pairs in bounded hash partitions. A pair is retained when its full sketch intersection meets the existing $MIN_IDENTITY^k$ threshold and has a collinear neighboring match. Overlapping windows on the same read are excluded as duplicates.
3. Candidate windows are merged into loci and clustered with the same best-partner Union-Find procedure as genome mode.
4. Candidate windows are merged into loci. For each input file, the modal non-singleton sampled k-mer multiplicity estimates haploid coverage C1, and the file's locus count N is converted to round(N / C1) before applying -c.

Output files

Both modes write the same merged-locus BED: #chrom, start, end, and cluster_id. Genome mode applies -c to each per-input-file locus count; read mode applies it to each count divided by that input file's estimated haploid coverage.
